A||||8||ThatRickGuy||Completely off the wall idea for turbo lag.||||3||
Z||000000||ThatRickGuy||08-22-2000||11:27 AM||||No idea what made me think of this, but I remember a lot of people talking about turbo lag at the line from the lower preasure in the exhaust. What if, you put a manual choke behind the turbo? Just befor take off, give it a yank, preasure builds, and right as you hit the gas, the turbo is already spun up. I don't even know if something like this would work, but it just poped into my head, thought I'd post it and see.||||reg||3
Z||000001||theogre||08-22-2000||01:43 PM||||Won't work. A turbo makes use of the pressure diferantial between the engine side and the outlet. It's that flow that makes it spin. You'd block that up with a valve down stream. The turbo would spin down not up when you shut the valve becuase it would have close to the same pressure on both sides. If you held it for more than a second you'd likely blow the exhaust apart or stall.<P>Properly sized turbo's generally don't lag much.||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||08-22-2000||02:50 PM||||James at Custom Network Motors (remaped the computer in my SE, also builder of the worlds only Twin Turbo CRX) has a patent on a CO2 injection. take 20oz. bottle of CO2 from a paintball gun or from NOS, a port drilled into the exhaust housing of the turbo @ 60* off the center of the wheel at the will of the driver at a starting tree at the strip you press a button the solenoids open giving you full boost (potential) and as soon as you drop the clutch and the waste gate senses a vacuum change the gate slams shut and BOOM! no lag and a full 15+ psi in an instant.  for more information on this e-mail james at <A HREF="http://www.engineered.net" TARGET=_blank>www.engineered.net</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||ThatRickGuy||08-22-2000||07:35 PM||||good call Ogre. Working helpdesk is a mind numbing job, everyonce and a while, my remaining 2 braincells get into a fight and I wind up with some stupid ideas. but that sounds like a sweet trick w/ the O2 bottle. I'd like to add turbo some day, but right now I'm scrounging for change to get my engine out of the shop, it's gonna be about $250 over the estimate! so, there goes the beer money.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||08-22-2000||08:29 PM||||I don't know about the newer cars, but some of the older cars fixed the turbo lag problem with 2 turbos.  One was small (spooled up quickly, but less oomph) and the other was larger (took longer to spool up, but made the power).  Would this work?||||reg||1
Z||000005||hugh||08-22-2000||11:49 PM||||If you are on the line ,you can bring up the boost while the line lock or brakes keep you from red lighting.You can leave under boost and not worry about lag.On the street if you get rid of all the restrictions in the intake before and after the turbo,such as the entrance to the throttle body,intercooler (if you have one),which is necessary if you want to keep detonation down,and the turbo inlet.Smoothing the flow pretty much eliminates turbo lag.A small turbo will be more responsive and spool up faster,a big turbo will spool slower but heat the compressed air less.||||reg||1
Z||000006||lou_dias||08-23-2000||12:25 AM||||Here's something it thought of years ago once I figured out how turbos and superchargers work...<P>Instead of supercharging your intake, do it to the exhaust to suck air out faster (and have a wastegate and second exhaust tube for a bypass when things are getting too hot) which would then be forced into the turbine and power the turbo induction which would then produce more exhaust gas that would get sucked out very quickly by the supercharged exhaust which would then power the turbine...you get the point...<P>Sounds interesting, doesn't it?||||reg||1
Z||000007||TOM||08-23-2000||01:28 AM||||there is a thing called back pressure. You need some. Thats why when guys go to really big exhaust without needing it they lose low end power.||||reg||1
Z||000008||FIEROCIOUS||08-23-2000||01:58 AM||||Tom, that's true for naturaly asperated cars but not turbo cars! Remember pinwheels as a child??? If you blow on the pinwheel it spins. If you put your hand behind the pinwheel it stops spinning. You need as little backpressure behind the wheel as posible!<BR>Back pressure is benificial only to the effect of keeping volocity high. ie, You wan't as little back pressure as possible without hurting volocity  for a N/A car.<P>Nology actualy make a exhaust choke if you will, it opens as the throttle is applied. This flatens the torque curve and makes torque higher across the RPM band.<P><BR>SPEED KiLLS DRiVE A HONDA AND LiVE FOREVER!||||reg||1
